## Project State Summary

**Problem:** go-unix-utils stress-tests a specification-driven code generation pipeline (cobbler/mage-claude-orchestrator) by reimplementing GNU utilities in Go. Specifications are derived from C source; Go implementations are verified via differential testing against Homebrew GNU reference binaries.

**Architecture:** Four-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify). `cmd/` holds per-utility Go packages, `pkg/` holds shared libraries (testutils, sys, format), `magefiles/` drives orchestration, `docs/` holds specs. Differential testing in `pkg/testutils/` is the gating verification mechanism.

**Current state:** 17 commands implemented across generations 1–17 (ts, head, seq, echo, tac, nl, fold, expand, unexpand, cut, paste, uniq, comm, md5sum, sha1sum, sha256sum, sha512sum plus earlier cat, wc, sponge). All code lives at generation-merged tags. The working branch has specs only. All releases from 00.0 through 05.4 show `codestatus: not started` on the current branch (code at tags). Release 99.2 (deferred ts) has status `spec_complete`.

**Current release scope:** Release 99.2 — the only use case is `rel99.2-uc001-ts` (ts utility, spec_complete, code not started on working branch).

## Priority Analysis

Release 99.2 contains a single use case: `rel99.2-uc001-ts`. The ts utility prepends timestamps to stdin lines (moreutils). The PRD, use case, and test suite exist. Code generation is needed. ts depends on `pkg/sys` (SIGPIPE handler) and `pkg/testutils` (differential testing), both of which are already implemented.

Since I am limited to 1 task and must split implementation from tests, I propose the implementation task for `cmd/ts`. The test task would follow in a subsequent session.

```yaml
- index: 0
  title: "cmd/ts implementation -- prd R1-R4 timestamp prepend, flags, format"
  dependency: -1
  description: |
    deliverable_type: code

    required_reading:
      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd005-ts.yaml (full PRD for ts utility)
      - docs/specs/use-cases/rel99.2-uc001-ts.yaml (use case for ts)
      - docs/specs/test-suites/test-rel-99.2.yaml (test suite for ts)
      - docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml (shared protocols section -- SIGPIPE handling, differential testing pattern)
      - pkg/sys/sigpipe.go (InstallSIGPIPEHandler usage)
      - cmd/head/main.go (reference for how other cmd/ utilities are structured)
      - cmd/seq/main.go (reference for flag parsing and main structure)

    files:
      - path: cmd/ts/main.go
        action: create
        note: ts utility entry point -- reads stdin, prepends timestamps, writes to stdout

    requirements:
      - id: R1
        text: |
          Read lines from stdin (or file arguments if specified in the PRD) and prepend
          a timestamp to each line before writing to stdout. Default format is the
          strftime-style format specified in the PRD (typically "%b %d %H:%M:%S").
          Accept an optional format string as a positional argument.
      - id: R2
        text: |
          Implement the -s flag for relative timestamps (elapsed time since the program
          started). When -s is active, the timestamp shows duration from start rather
          than wall-clock time. Use the format specified in the PRD for relative mode.
      - id: R3
        text: |
          Implement the -i flag for incremental timestamps (time elapsed since the
          previous line was output). First line shows time since program start.
          Use the format specified in the PRD for incremental mode.
      - id: R4
        text: |
          Implement the -m flag to use a monotonic clock source instead of wall-clock
          time. This flag modifies the behavior of -s and -i to use monotonic time.
          Support %.S or equivalent sub-second precision in format strings as specified
          in the PRD.

    design_decisions:
      - id: D1
        text: |
          Call pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() at the start of main() per the shared
          protocol in ARCHITECTURE.yaml. This ensures ts exits cleanly when piped to
          a consumer that closes stdin early (e.g., head).
      - id: D2
        text: |
          Follow the same cmd/ package structure as existing utilities (head, seq, echo).
          Single main.go file with flag parsing via the flag package, reading stdin with
          bufio.Scanner, and writing to os.Stdout.
      - id: D3
        text: |
          The reference binary for differential testing is the moreutils ts installed
          via Homebrew. The binary name is "ts" (moreutils does not use g-prefix).
          Do NOT install any packages in the build or test process. The stitch agent
          must not attempt to install moreutils.
      - id: D4
        text: |
          Read the PRD carefully for the exact strftime format conversion rules. Go
          uses time.Format with reference-time layout strings, not strftime. Map
          strftime directives (%Y, %m, %d, %H, %M, %S, etc.) to Go equivalents.
          Handle %.S sub-second precision as specified in the PRD.

    acceptance_criteria:
      - id: AC1
        text: cmd/ts/main.go compiles with "go build ./cmd/ts/" and produces a working binary.
      - id: AC2
        text: |
          Running "echo hello | bin/ts" prepends a timestamp in the default format to
          the output line.
      - id: AC3
        text: |
          The -s flag produces relative timestamps from program start, and the -i flag
          produces incremental timestamps between lines.
      - id: AC4
        text: |
          The -m flag switches to monotonic clock for -s and -i modes.
      - id: AC5
        text: |
          A custom format string argument (e.g., "%H:%M:%S") is applied to the timestamp
          output.
      - id: AC6
        text: |
          pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() is called at the start of main().
      - id: AC7
        text: |
          "go vet ./cmd/ts/" reports no issues.
```